    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In Singapore, when evaluating the \"unforeseeability\" of an event for the purposes of a force majeure clause, courts will consider whether the event was truly unforeseeable at the time the lease was entered into. The key factor is the knowledge and expectations of the parties at that time, rather than hindsight.\n\nIf a similar disruption occurred a decade ago, it would not necessarily prevent the event from being considered unforeseeable if the circumstances were significantly different or if the risk of such an event was not reasonably anticipated. For example, the COVID-19 pandemic was an unprecedented global event with unique characteristics that were not foreseeable based on past experiences.\n\nCourts will examine the specific language of the force majeure clause, the nature of the event, and the context in which the lease was signed. They will also consider whether the event was truly beyond the control of the parties and whether it directly prevented the tenant from fulfilling their obligations under the lease.\n\nIn cases where the event was not explicitly covered by the force majeure clause, the tenant may still argue that the event was unforeseeable and that it should be treated as a force majeure event under the doctrine of frustration. However, this would require a separate legal analysis and may not be as straightforward as invoking a",
